nd CPO is forced to 3.3 V rather than acting as a charge-pump node.\", \"confidence\": 0.7}, {\"category\": \"open_connection\", \"components\": [\"U1\"], \"pins\": [\"U1.2\"], \"nets\": [\"unconnected-(U1-EN-Pad2)\"], \"observed\": \"The EN pin (pin 2) of the TPS62933 buck regulator U1 is marked no-connect and is not tied to VIN or to any enable net.\", \"expected\": \"A TPS62933 enable input should be defined by a resistor divider from VIN or a pull-up, not left floating, so that the regulator starts reliably from the 24 V rail.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"135\\\") (name \\\"unconnected-(U1-EN-Pad2)\\\") (class \\\"Default\\\") (node (ref \\\"U1\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pinfunction \\\"EN\\\") (pintype \\\"input+no_connect\\\")))\"}], \"impact\": \"The buck regulator's enable behaviour depends on an undefined input, so the +5V rail may fail to start or may start unpredictably. The reference design may do the same, so this finding is low confidence.\", \"confidence\": 0.2}], \"summary\": \"The NB stepper driver U7 has its charge-pump output CPO tied to the +3V3 rail, and its charge-pump capacitor C48 sits between +3V3 and CPI, unlike the matching NA and pH drivers. This breaks the TMC2209 charge pump and disables the NB pump's high-side drive. A secondary, lower-confidence concern is the floating EN pin on the buck regulator U1.\"}",
